why: Batch. The questions that settle the division of hands: who answers, who does, who checks, and what the clerk gives first. A blank reply does not release a line.

cite: 3.1

Overview:
You named three pieces: mechanical, model, man. Mechanical is compute. Every act needs to know its calling so the app knows who is responsible and where.
Face `04` holds a first proposal: a table of acts, with which hand answers, which does, and which checks. These lines ask you to settle it piece by piece.
`#01` and `#02` set the frame. `#03` to `#09` say what each hand may answer. `#10` and `#11` are the order and the hand-over. `#12` to `#14` are where it is written and where it lives.
Learned:
Law `0001` already gives each section an owner class (kernel-only, desk-local, book-local). That says who authorized a rule. The calling is a different question: which hand acts and which answers. They should not share a name.
Approve shows the shape: the person answers, compute does the rename, compute checks. One act, three hands.
How: One question is one #NN. The recommend and the Ask name the same command. Your reply may take, change, hold or reject.
Scale: here, how much goes wrong if the default is wrong. 1 work could be lost or authority taken. 2 a stage is redone. 3 a batch is redone. 4 wording or taste. 5 nothing breaks.

#01 >decide the names of the three hands
placed: 2026-10-17
issue: You wrote mechanical, model, man. The kernel says "human directs. model reasons. compute maintains." (axiom 3). The plan says clerk, prime, Director. One set of words should name the hands everywhere, and the person hand needs a name that also fits a user's own desk.
recommend: I think we should >take compute, model, person. Compute is the clerk. Model covers the prime, workers and colabs. Person is whoever holds the desk: on this desk, the Director.
